    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d355">

                <pb facs="0582" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15 Arms Truce/60: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berne</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-02T13:00:00+01:00">October 2, 1931—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 2—11:55 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>177. Your 133, October 1, 11 a.m. The authors of the two proposals (Italian and
                    Scandinavian) were united on the point that construction already begun could be
                    carried to its completion.</p>
                <pb facs="0583" n="469" xml:id="pg469"/>
                <p>In the eighth meeting of the Third Committee on September 21 the Italian delegate
                    said “vessels under construction could be continued and completed”.<anchor
                        n="70" xml:id="d355-f1"/> In the ninth meeting on September 22nd the Danish
                    delegate said “the expression ‘not to increase the present level of armaments’
                    did not imply that work under consideration would be stopped”.<anchor n="71"
                        xml:id="d355-f2"/> Also see report in my 171, September 29, 11 a.m., in that
                    portion dealing with Italian proposal point 2 reading as follows:</p>
                <p>“Not to place any warship on the stocks provided always that vessels under
                    construction might be continued and completed”.</p>
                <p>[Paraphrase.] There are no minutes of the subcommittee in which the greater part
                    of the debate took place, but at no time was there any doubt cast on the right
                    of a state to complete construction already begun. Frequently it was stated that
                    this right would be unaffected by any agreement reached. [End paraphrase.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
